e point let's just say  let's say it's 2.5 standard deviations  above the mean let's say it's right  there what the table will do is will  give you the percentage that are at that  value or below so it's always going to  give you this area of this  it's to the left of that z-score or that  number of standard deviations so if you  wanted to calculate the amount above  since this area underneath this curve is  1 or you could think of it as a hundred  percent you would have to actually do 1  minus this area to get this little area  above that to the right okay so that's  something to be aware of so again X is  our data point x-bar is the mean and  then Sigma is our standard deviation so  let's look at an example to see how we  work with this so say that the average  height of a student in your school is 65  inches okay with the standard deviation  of three inches find the probability  that if you pick somebody at random that  their height is less than or equal to 60  inches okay so what we're going to do is  we're going to go ahead and use our  z-score formula we have Z equals okay 60  which is our data point minus the mean  which is 65 that's the average height of  a student divided by 3 which is our  standard deviation so if we calculate  that out we get let's see negative 5/3  okay which is approximately let's see  negative one point seven if we round to  the tenth okay so what you can do is you  can take a look at a standard normal  distribution table and if I go to  negative one point seven okay I can see  that it's point zero four four six okay  so this is point zero four four six so  what that means is if we go over here to  negative one point seven right there  okay the area to the left of that line  okay this part of the curve that area is  going to be about four point five  percent okay so point zero four four six  if you convert that to a percentage  that's about four point five percent so  the probability would be or the chance  would be four and a half percent that  somebody would be lower than that height  or shorter than that height okay let's  go to the next one say that the  probability that you pick somebody at  random that they're greater or taller  than seventy inches so same formula Z  equals 70 - 65 the mean divided by the  standard deviation here we're getting a  proc  ly+ 1.7 okay so if we go over here to  1.7 that's gonna be right about here  okay and we're trying to find the  probability that somebody is tall or  greater so meaning to the right okay now  if we go to our standard normal  distribution table or chart you can see  here that it's going to be about point  nine five five four okay so point nine  five five four  but that's giving you the area to the  left  okay below we want the area that's above  to the right so what we have to do is we  have to do one minus point nine five  five four and we get approximately point  zero four four six okay which is again  about four point five percent chance  that somebody's going to be taller okay  than 70 inches okay so they're with me  so far  okay last example so you want to find  the probability that you're in between  sixty inches and seventy inches tall  okay so what you would do is you would  calculate the two z-scores like we did  here but you're trying to find you know  let's draw this curve again you're  trying to find the probably that you're  in between here and here okay so this is  represents 1.7 standard deviations above  the mean  okay the mean is right down the middle  and negative 1.7 standard deviations  below the mean okay and we're trying to  find the area here in between those well  the way that the charts work is that  it's giving you the area below so when  we do positive 1.7 it's giving us this  area 0.95 five four okay which we got  from the last problem what we have to do  is we have to subtract off this area  here to give us the leftover so we're  going to take the point nine five five  four minus this area here which we got  was point zero four four six and that's  going to be this leftover area here  which I did that calculation for us here  and that comes out to 0.9 one zero eight  point nine one zero eight so that means  it's about a ninety-one percent chance  that somebody's going to be you know  greater than sixty inches but less than  70 inches so I hope this video helps you  understand how to work with z-scores and  standard normal distributions if you're  enjoying these videos and you're  learning something please subscribe to  the channel check out some of my past  videos and I've got more videos coming  like this for you so I look forward to  seeing you in the future videos I'll  talk to you soon
 

